From 86c5ae9f8b2a2449f6e9d24c442cca2ff76febc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Takashi SASAKI <takashi316@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 10 Jul 2026 13:10:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] fix(visual-analysis): validate bundle exports and correct
 cross-provider evaluation

Add a shared typed Analysis Bundle retrieval helper that fetches through the application endpoint, rejects AI Studio cookie-check and HTML responses, validates supported bundle schema versions, checks structural fields, and enforces the requested job ID before copy or download actions can serialize data.

Route server-job copy and download actions through the validated helper so direct browser navigation can no longer save authentication wrapper HTML as a JSON bundle. Download now creates a JSON Blob from validated bundle data, uses the validated job ID in the sanitized filename, and revokes the object URL after triggering the save.

Correct cross-provider evaluation reporting by recomputing retained legacy review failure counters from actual review statuses, marking generation failures as review not-applicable, surfacing counter consistency in invariants, and updating the analysis guidance ordering toward artifact integrity and stage-specific aggregates.

Harden public-sample label comparison by ignoring generic one-token overlaps, requiring head-noun or stronger multi-token evidence, and exposing match confidence while preserving valid substring/head-noun matches such as desks and drawer pulls.

Improve package-image prompt guidance to require the whole primary package/container separately from labels and regions, retry provider INTERNAL/HTTP 500 failures once by default with bounded diagnostics, and preserve provider-safe JPEG/PNG bytes when images already satisfy size and dimension caps.

Keep provider-support metadata separate from visual output quality, add providerInternalError diagnostic compatibility, and extend focused regression coverage for bundle retrieval, review counters, comparator matching, internal-error retry behavior, prompt/image-processing behavior, and bundle sanitization.
